# rules: java-stream-never-closed
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-772.java
# from: CWE-772 (Missing Release of Resource), CWE-404
#
# A stream holds a file descriptor, and a descriptor is not memory, so
# nothing about running out of them makes the garbage collector run. A server
# that leaks one per request stops being able to open files or accept
# connections after a few thousand requests, and the failure surfaces as
# `Too many open files` somewhere unrelated.
#
# Java's answer is try-with-resources, and the grammar gives it a node of its
# own: a resource declared in a `try (...)` header is not a
# local_variable_declaration, so the pattern here does not match it and no
# subtraction is needed for the correct spelling. That is a rare case of the
# safe form being invisible to the rule by construction rather than by a
# guard, and it is why this rule is short.
#
# A wrapper is not a resource. `new InputStreamReader(input, charset)` and
# `new BufferedWriter(out)` hold no descriptor of their own - they hold
# somebody else's, and closing one closes the stream its caller owns, which in
# a copy utility is precisely the bug. commons-io's CopyUtils is six methods
# of exactly that shape and the first version of this rule reported all six.
#
# So the wrapper types are matched only where their argument opens something
# fresh: `new BufferedReader(new FileReader(f))` is one resource written as
# two constructors and is reported, and `new BufferedReader(reader)` is not
# reported at all. The test is a regex over the argument rather than a nested
# pattern because what it asks is "does this open a descriptor", which is a
# question about a handful of class names and not about structure.
#
# Two more things are subtracted, and both are the reading that says "someone
# else is dealing with it":
#
#   - a method that mentions `close` anywhere. Coarse on purpose - it covers
#     the `finally` blocks written before try-with-resources existed, and
#     `IOUtils.closeQuietly`, and a close inside a branch. See "The function
#     body as text".
#   - a stream that is returned. It belongs to the caller now. This is a
#     second, strictly longer reading of the same declaration with the return
#     spliced onto it, focused onto the declared name so that the two spans
#     coincide and the subtraction lands - see "focus, job two".
#
# The declaration and the return have to be siblings for one pattern to see
# both, so a stream returned out of an `if` is still reported. That is the
# usual limit of a two-statement reading and it costs a false positive rather
# than a missed leak.
#
# Only a stream declared inside a method is asked about. A `$T $V = $E;`
# pattern now reads a field as well as a local, which is what makes
# java-credential-is-a-literal possible - but a field holding a stream is a
# different question, because its lifetime is the object's and whatever closes
# it is somewhere this rule cannot see. `within` the method declarations is how
# the rule says "a local", and it costs the stream opened in a constructor or
# an initialiser block, neither of which is a method_declaration.

["FileInputStream", "FileOutputStream", "FileReader", "FileWriter",
 "RandomAccessFile", "Socket", "ServerSocket", "ZipFile", "JarFile"] as $opens
| ["BufferedReader", "BufferedWriter", "PrintWriter", "Scanner",
   "InputStreamReader", "OutputStreamWriter",
   "ObjectInputStream", "ObjectOutputStream"] as $wraps
| ($opens | map("$T $V = new " + . + "($$$_);")
   + ["$T $V = DriverManager.getConnection($$$_);"]) as $opened
| ($wraps | map("$T $V = new " + . + "($ARG);")
   + ($wraps | map("$T $V = new " + . + "($ARG, $$$_);"))) as $wrapped
| "^new\\s+(?:File(?:Input|Output)Stream|FileReader|FileWriter|"
  + "RandomAccessFile|Socket|ZipFile|JarFile)\\s*\\(|^\"" as $fresh
| (($opened + $wrapped) | map(. + "\n$$$_\nreturn $R;")) as $returned
| ["$RT $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| "\\.close\\s*\\(|closeQuietly|closeSilently|IOUtils\\.close" as $closes
| scan_ast("*.java"; $opened + $wrapped + $returned + $methods) as $all
| ( ( ($all | of($opened) | focus("V"))
      + ($all | of($wrapped) | where_capture("ARG"; $fresh) | focus("V")) )
    | within($all | of($methods))
    | not_at($all | of($returned) | where_same("V"; "R") | focus("V"))
    | outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $closes)) )
| finding("java-stream-never-closed";
    "nothing here closes $V, so it holds its descriptor until the process "
    + "ends; try-with-resources closes it on every path out")
| report
